          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been diagnosed with high blood pressure.",
          "answer": "Being told you have high blood pressure can raise a lot of questions. Here is what the guidance we searched says. It does not tell us what applies to you.\n\n**How blood pressure is classified.** US cardiology guidance (Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets) sorts adult office readings into four groups: normal, elevated, stage 1 and stage 2. Stage 1 means the top number is 130 to 139, or the bottom number is 80 to 89. Stage 2 means the top number is 140 or more, or the bottom number is 90 or more. If the two numbers fall in different groups, the higher group applies.\n\n**When treatment usually starts.** The treatment guidance (Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line) says that for some people, such as those with established heart or blood-vessel disease, diabetes, chronic kidney disease, or a 10-year cardiovascular risk of 10 percent or more, medicine is often started alongside lifestyle changes. For others with stage 1 readings, lifestyle changes come first and the blood pressure is rechecked after three to six months. That passage was cut short in our search, so we could not confirm the full criteria.\n\n**Common treatment groups.** The same document lists four drug classes that are first-line for most adults. These are thiazide-type diuretics, ACE inhibitors, angiotensin receptor blockers and dihydropyridine calcium-channel blockers. Which group, if any, suits a particular person depends on their health history, and that is a decision for their clinician.\n\n**Gaps.** Our search did not return the blood-pressure target guidance in full, so we cannot tell you a specific target from these sources. The documents also flag areas where experts disagree, and we did not retrieve those sections. We have no information about your readings, your other health conditions or your medicines.\n\n**Useful questions for your clinician:**\n- Which category do my readings fall into, and how sure are we of that?\n- Do I need medicine now, or lifestyle changes first with a recheck?\n- What blood pressure target should I aim for, given my other health factors?\n- Are there any risks I should watch for with the treatment you recommend?\n\nIf you ever have chest pain, trouble breathing, sudden weakness, numbness, confusion, trouble speaking, or a severe headache with vision changes, call your local emergency number right away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
