          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What about diet?",
          "answer": "Here is what the diet guidance we searched says. It does not tell us what applies to you.\n\n**The main eating pattern.** The blood-pressure diet guidance (Diet for high blood pressure) describes a whole eating pattern rather than one \"blood pressure food.\" It emphasizes vegetables, fruit, whole grains, low-fat dairy, poultry, fish, beans, nuts and vegetable oils. It limits saturated fat, full-fat dairy, fatty meat, sweets and sugar-sweetened drinks. The document says cutting sodium (salt) on top of this pattern gives a larger effect than either change alone.\n\n**Sodium in packaged and restaurant food.** A related heart-failure diet note (Diet and fluid in heart failure) says most dietary sodium comes from packaged and restaurant food, not from the salt shaker. That note is about a different condition, so its advice should not be applied to you without your clinician.\n\n**Diet is not one-size-fits-all.** The diabetes diet guidance (Diet for type 2 diabetes) says several eating patterns have evidence behind them, and the best one is the one a person can keep up with, given their tastes, culture, budget and cooking. This is a general point, not a statement about your situation.\n\n**Physical activity.** The activity guidance (Physical activity for high blood pressure) gives a commonly stated target of 150 minutes a week of moderate activity, such as brisk walking, or 75 minutes of vigorous activity, plus muscle-strengthening on two or more days. It says the aerobic part carries most of the blood-pressure benefit.\n\n**Gaps.** Our search did not return specific sodium limits, portion guidance, or advice on how diet interacts with any medicine you might take. We have no information about your weight, other conditions or medicines. Before changing your diet in a significant way, especially if you take medicine or have kidney or heart problems, talk with your clinician or a dietitian.\n\n**Questions you could bring to your clinician:**\n- How much sodium should I aim for each day?\n- Which changes to my diet would matter most for me?\n- Are there foods or supplements I should avoid with my treatment?\n- Should I see a dietitian?\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
